      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's denote the unknown side of the triangle as \\( x \\) cm. According to the given information, the triangle is a right triangle with sides of 6 cm, 8 cm, and \\( x \\) cm. We can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the value of \\( x \\).\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. In this case, the hypotenuse is the side we are trying to find, so we can write the equation as:\n\n\\[ 6^2 + 8^2 = x^2 \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 36 + 64 = x^2 \\]\n\\[ 100 = x^2 \\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we find:\n\n\\[ x = \\sqrt{100} \\]\n\\[ x = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is \\( \\boxed{10.00} \\) cm.",
